## Figma design requirements

When creating or modifying Figma designs:

1. **Name layers semantically** — reflect heading level, button, input, nav (maps to 1.3.1, 4.1.2).
2. **Annotate** contrast ratios on text/color pairs; flag any <4.5:1 (1.4.3) or <3:1 for UI shapes (1.4.11).
3. **Show focus states** as a variant for every interactive component (2.4.7, 2.4.11).
4. **Size touch targets** ≥24px (annotate 44px as best practice) (2.5.8).
5. **Never indicate state by color alone** — show icon + label variants (1.4.1).
6. **Prototype keyboard paths** — tab order annotation layer (2.4.3).
7. **Reduced motion variant** — static state alternative (2.3.3).
8. **Export specs** must include: accessible name, role, state, error text, and focus behaviour.

---

## Testing guidance

When verifying compliance, check:

1. **Keyboard-only** — Tab through entire flow; all actions reachable; focus visible; no traps.
2. **Screen reader** — VoiceOver (macOS/iOS) or NVDA (Windows): names, roles, states, live regions announced.
3. **Zoom** — 200% and 400% browser zoom; 320px viewport reflow.
4. **Contrast** — Measure with DevTools or APCA; verify token pairs in both light and dark appearance.
5. **Automated** — axe-core / Lighthouse accessibility audit; fix all critical/serious issues.
6. **Reduced motion** — Enable OS setting; animations must stop or substitute.

---

## Priority when fixing violations

1. **Blocking (A/AA + EAA)** — Fix before delivering. Flag to user if you cannot fix in scope.
2. **AAA recommendations** — Document as improvements; implement when low-cost.
3. **EAA organisational** (statements, training, help desk) — Flag for product/legal team; not code-fixable alone.

